{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgCp3SWKtLvbqEUXpudQo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The O3 helper is `openmrsFetch`, defined in `openmrs-esm-core`. It is a thin wrapper around `window.fetch`. It does not throw on HTTP errors itself. Instead it handles auth failures with a configurable redirect to the login page, and rejects other non-2xx responses with an `OpenmrsFetchError`.\n\n**Authentication failures**\n\n- Redirect rule: a response triggers the auth-failure path when the request is to the session endpoint with status 403, or when its status is in `redirectAuthFailure.errors`. The default is `[401]`, and `redirectAuthFailure.enabled` defaults to `true` (`openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts:226-229`, `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/config-schema.ts:6-27`).\n- What happens: the history is cleared and the app navigates to `redirectAuthFailure.url`. If that is blank, it uses the response's `Location` header, then the default `${openmrsSpaBase}/login` (`openmrs-fetch.ts:230-235`).\n- Promise behaviour: by default the returned promise never settles, so callers do not see an error. `redirectAuthFailure.resolvePromise` can make it resolve instead. If the caller passes `rejectAuthFailure: true`, the promise rejects with `OpenmrsFetchError` after the navigation (`openmrs-fetch.ts:237-242`, `openmrs-fetch.ts:285-293`).\n- Redirect headers: on REST requests, `Disable-WWW-Authenticate: true` is added unless the caller overrides it, so the server does not send a browser auth challenge (`openmrs-fetch.ts:137-163`). The tests cover the Location-header and login-URL cases (`openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.test.ts:166-241`).\n\n**Other error responses**\n\n- Non-2xx responses that are not auth redirects are read as text and parsed as JSON when possible. The helper then throws `OpenmrsFetchError` with `response`, `responseBody`, and a combined stack trace of the request and response (`openmrs-fetch.ts:245-268`, `openmrs-fetch.ts:272-283`).\n- 204 responses return `data: null`. Successful responses get `data` set from the parsed body (`openmrs-fetch.ts:197-218`).\n\n**Consumers**\n\n- `current-user.ts` uses `isAuthFailure`, which treats a 401 or 403 `OpenmrsFetchError` as an authentication failure. It is used for session handling such as logout, where an already-ended session counts as success (`openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/current-user.ts:539`, `current-user.ts:604-606`). I did not read the surrounding code in that file.\n\n**Documentation gap**\n\nThe public doc page `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/openmrsFetch.md` is generated from the JSDoc. It says nothing about auth redirects or `OpenmrsFetchError`. The behaviour is documented in the source comment (`openmrs-fetch.ts:40-88`), the `FetchConfig.rejectAuthFailure` comment, and the `redirectAuthFailure` config descriptions.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts\", \"startLine\": 89, \"endLine\": 270},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts\", \"startLine\": 219, \"endLine\": 243},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts\", \"startLine\": 272, \"endLine\": 283},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts\", \"startLine\": 285, \"endLine\": 293},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts\", \"startLine\": 40, \"endLine\": 88},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts\", \"startLine\": 137, \"endLine\": 163},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/openmrs-fetch.ts\", \"startLine\": 179, \"endLine\": 218},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-api/src/config-schema.ts\", \"startLine\": 5, \"endL